# 2. Using --fixup and autosquash(creating a new commit which squashes into our old one)

---

    Step 1: Make the changes
    # edit auth.js
    git add auth.js
    Step 2: Create a fixup commit
    git commit --fixup <hash-of-C>

    Suppose C's hash is a1b2c3:

    git commit --fixup a1b2c3

    Git creates a new commit like:

    fixup! Add login page

    History becomes:

    A
    B
    C
    D
    E
    F  fixup! Add login page

    Notice that nothing has been rewritten yet. You've simply added a new commit.

    Step 3: Autosquash

    Run:

    git rebase -i --autosquash HEAD~4

    Git automatically changes the rebase plan from:

    pick C
    pick D
    pick E
    pick fixup! Add login page

    to:

    pick C
    fixup fixup! Add login page
    pick D
    pick E

    You don't have to move the fixup commit manually.

    Save and exit.

    Result:

    A
    B
    C'
    D'
    E'

    The changes from F are folded into C, and the fixup! commit disappears.

    # Update the remote branch if you've already pushed
    git push --force-with-lease

